  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Advancing Women&#x2019;s Rights of Access to Information in Bangladesh (AWRTI-2)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">(Bangladesh):  In Bangladesh, the most marginalized populations face restricted access to information, with women disproportionately affected. Corruption thrives in an environment characterized by secrecy, lack of transparency, weak accountability, and barriers to information, which fuels corruption. USAID&apos;s Advancing Women&apos;s Rights to Access Information in Bangladesh (AWRTI-2) activity promotes access to information as a means to combat corruption. This initiative will empower citizens to file requests for non-controversial data, thereby building a framework for transparent governance.With FY 2024 funds, the activity will provide training to women in the community on how to access government-held information and work with local communities to raise awareness about the challenges women face when exercising their right to access information. The program will also collaborate with civil society organizations focused on women&apos;s rights and economic empowerment to enhance women&apos;s understanding of the 2009 Right to Information Act (RTI). AWRTI-2 will also partner with Government of Bangladesh ministries to train designated information officers, aiming to mitigate gender disparities in information dissemination practices and enhance their capacity for effective RTI implementation. The initiative will ensure gender-sensitive implementation of the RTI law, making the government more transparent and open to public scrutiny, which is expected to strengthen anti-corruption efforts.It will create an evidence base for the government to develop better-informed policies that contribute to the Integrated Country Strategy Mission Objective 2.2 - Citizen confidence in governance institutions increases. The overall goal of reducing corruption through increased public-led accountability directly aligns with USAID&apos;s CDCS MO1 - Democracy strengthened for an inclusive society. This IM contributes to the Women&apos;s Leadership earmark ($200k) and Anti-Corruption ($300k).</narrative>
  </description>
